("Advanced",
 "High-Volume Streaming Exchange",
 "Batch exchange moves yesterday's data tomorrow; streaming moves today's data now. Platforms like Apache Kafka provide the pattern: append-only partitioned logs, producers writing events, consumers reading at their own pace, retention measured in days, replay for recovery. The exchange contract covers event schemas (registered and versioned), partitioning keys (what order means), delivery semantics (at-least-once typical, exactly-once with effort), and consumer lag monitoring. Streaming demands schema discipline - a bad event poisons every consumer - and operational maturity: lag alerts, dead-letter queues, and capacity planning. Use streaming when freshness is the requirement, not the luxury.",
 ["Append-only partitioned logs; producers write, consumers read at own pace",
  "Schemas registered and versioned; partitioning keys define ordering",
  "Delivery semantics explicit: at-least-once typical, exactly-once with effort",
  "Monitor consumer lag; dead-letter queues for poison events",
  "Streaming when freshness is required; batch when it isn't"],
 "A streaming platform with schema registry, lag monitoring, and dead-letter handling.",
 "dx_csv2json"),

("Advanced",
 "FHIR: Healthcare Exchange Case Study",
 "HL7 FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) shows domain interoperability done at scale: health data modeled as Resources (Patient, Observation, Medication) with RESTful APIs, JSON/XML representations, and extensibility without breaking the base. FHIR's maturity model grades each resource's stability; its profiles constrain resources for national regulations; SMART on FHIR adds app authorization. The lessons generalize: start from implementer pain, standardize the 80 percent common case, profile the rest, version openly, and test at connectathons. FHIR succeeded where predecessors stalled by being buildable - developers could implement it in a weekend. Interoperability standards win by being usable, not by being complete.",
 ["Health data as Resources with RESTful APIs and JSON/XML",
  "Profiles constrain resources for national regulations",
  "Maturity model grades stability; connectathons prove interop",
  "Standardize the 80 percent; profile the rest",
  "Standards win by being usable, not by being complete"],
 "FHIR servers with national profiles and connectathon-tested implementations (in healthcare).",
 "dx_openapi"),

("Advanced",
 "The Interoperability Test Suite: Proving It Works",
 "Claims of interoperability are cheap; test suites make them checkable. A conformance test suite encodes the standard as executable assertions: valid documents accepted, invalid documents rejected with the right errors, edge cases probed, round-trips verified. Reference implementations provide the baseline behavior; plugfests and connectathons let vendors test against each other before customers do. Certification programs turn passing into a market signal. The suite is versioned with the standard and runnable in CI, so every release proves conformance anew. In interoperability, the test suite IS the standard's enforcement arm - without it, 'compliant' is a marketing word.",
 ["Executable assertions: valid accepted, invalid rejected correctly",
  "Reference implementations set baseline behavior",
  "Connectathons test vendors against each other pre-market",
  "Suite versioned with the standard, run in CI per release",
  "Without tests, 'compliant' is marketing"],
 "A versioned conformance suite run in CI plus connectathon participation.",
 "dx_apicontract"),
